<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles Alexandre de Calonne</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1734">1734</a>–<a href="/years/1802">1802</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles Alexandre de Calonne was a French statesman who served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi">Louis XVI</a>'s controller-general of finances from 1783 to 1787. Faced with a growing deficit, he proposed a general land tax paid by all landowners, including the privileged, and summoned an Assembly of Notables in 1787 to approve it. The notables refused, he was dismissed, and the crisis led to the calling of the Estates-General in 1789.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-alexandre-de-calonne.jpg" alt="Charles Alexandre de Calonne" width="320" height="383" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Élisabeth Louise Vigée Le Brun · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vig%C3%A9e-Lebrun,_Elisabeth-Louise_-_Charles-Alexandre_de_Calonne_(1734-1802)_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
